<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glucose and glucose-1-phosphate degradation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="GLUCOSE1PMETAB-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; It is well known that |FRAME:TAX-562| can use glucose as a sole source of carbon and energy. The D isomer of glucose is widely found in nature and |FRAME: Glucopyranose| is predominant in aqueous solution |CITS: [FRANKS87]|. Exogenous |FRAME: Glucopyranose| enters the cell through outer membrane porins and is then actively transported into the cell via the inner membrane phosphotransferase system (PTS) which transforms it into |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate| as it crosses the cell membrane. |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate| is also produced biosynthetically during gluconeogenesis. |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate| is one of the basic precursor metabolites for biosynthetic pathways. It is also a substrate for the central degradative pathways glycolysis and the pentose phosphate cycle. |FRAME:TAX-562| can also grow on exogenously supplied glucose-1-phosphate (minimal medium containing glucose 1-phosphate) as sole carbon source |CITS: [1648777]|. Endogenous |FRAME: GLC-1-P| is an intermediate in the metabolism of |FRAME: Glycogens glycogen| and |FRAME: D-Galactose|. It is a building block for the sugar nucleotide |FRAME: CPD-12575|, which is used in some biosynthetic pathways. Reviewed by Mayer, C. and W. Boos in |CITS: [ecosal]| (see below). &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Substrates |FRAME: Glucopyranose| and |FRAME: GLC-1-P| may be derived from exogenous sources, or endogenously produced, as indicated by the input pathway links. In general, the ability to utilize sugars and their modes of utilization are strain-dependent in |FRAME:TAX-562|. Exogenous |FRAME: GLC| uptake via the PTS curbs the utilization of other exogenous sugars, which is known as the glucose effect. This effect is lost if |FRAME: GLC| becomes limiting. Under these conditions |FRAME: GLC| can also enter the cell without phosphorylation, via outer membrane porins and the Mgl ABC transporter (not shown). Endogenous |FRAME: GLC| can be produced by pathways for the degradation of glucose-containing disaccharides such as |FRAME: MALTOSE| (see pathway |FRAME: GLYCOCAT-PWY|) |FRAME: TREHALOSE|, |FRAME: Alpha-lactose| and |FRAME: MELIBIOSE|, as shown in the pathway links. In contrast to exogenous |FRAME: GLC| which is phosphorylated by the PTS, endogenous |FRAME: GLC| is phosphorylated by glucokinase before entering central metabolism, as shown in the pathway links (in |CITS: [9023215]|). More recently, a role for glucokinase and glucose in a complex regulatory mechanism for maltose utilization involving Glk, MalT, Mlc and PtsG has been proposed |CITS: [19028900]|. It is possible that high levels of |FRAME: GLC| could accumulate inside the cell under certain conditions. It has been shown that the maltose acetyltransferase product of gene &lt;i&gt;maa&lt;/i&gt; efficiently acetylates both maltose and |FRAME: GLC| (not shown). Evidence suggests that acetylation could be a detoxification mechanism in which acetylated |FRAME: GLC| diffuses from the cell |CITS: [7012137] [1856235]|. There is evidence that |FRAME: GLC| can be oxidized to |FRAME: GLC-D-LACTONE| (glucono-&amp;delta;-lactone) by inner membrane glucose dehydrogenase. However, the fate of the |FRAME: GLC-D-LACTONE| remains unclear. It has been reported that membrane vesicles from glucose-grown |FRAME:TAX-562| oxidized glucose to gluconate in the presence of |FRAME: PQQ|, a cofactor for glucose dehydrogenase |CITS: [3926746]|. A gluconolactonase (EC 3.1.1.17) has been partially characterized in |FRAME:TAX-562|, but its |FRAME: GLUCONATE| product was not specifically identified |CITS: [4625870]| and no gene encoding this enzyme has been identified. |FRAME: GLUCONATE| can be degraded by a glucose utilization pathway that was described early |CITS: [14882337]|, as shown in the pathway link. In addition, more recent work suggested possible excretion of |FRAME: GLUCONATE| although this compound was not specifically identified |CITS: [20215780]|. |FRAME: GLC-1-P| is reversibly converted by phosphoglucomutase to |FRAME: ALPHA-GLC-6-P|. |FRAME: GLC-1-P| is used in glycogen biosynthesis (see |FRAME: GLYCOGENSYNTH-PWY|) and is produced during glycogen degradation (see |FRAME: GLYCOCAT-PWY|). |FRAME: ALPHA-GLC-6-P| may spontaneously convert to |FRAME: GLC-6-P| in the physiological pH range |CITS: [14275652]|. In addition, a glucose-6-phosphate 1-epimerase had been identified in |FRAME:TAX-562| ATCC 9637 that could catalyze this production of |FRAME: GLC-6-P|. Several phosphatases may catalyze the production of |FRAME: D-Glucose| (anomeric form unspecified) from |FRAME: GLC-1-P|. The product of gene &lt;i&gt;agp&lt;/i&gt; is a periplasmic enzyme that scavenges glucose and allows |FRAME:TAX-562| to grow with glucose-1-phosphate as sole carbon source |CITS: [1648777]| and in |CITS: [12782623]|. Reviewed in Mayer, C. and W. Boos (2005) &quot;Hexose/Pentose and Hexitol/Pentitol Metabolism.&quot; EcoSal module 3.4.1 |CITS: [ecosal]|.</Description>
